    "January": "January",
    "Korea's largest spring festival, attracting millions of visitors who walk under tunnels of over 360,000 blooming cherry blossom trees. The Yeojwacheon Stream and Gyeonghwa Station railway are famous photo spots.": "Korea's largest spring festival, attracting millions of visitors who walk under tunnels of over 360,000 blooming cherry blossom trees. The Yeojwacheon Stream and Gyeonghwa Station railway are famous photo spots.",
    "An energetic, wild summer party where locals and tourists slather themselves in mineral-rich mud known for its cosmetic properties. Expect giant mud slides, mud wrestling, beach concerts, and firework shows.": "An energetic, wild summer party where locals and tourists slather themselves in mineral-rich mud known for its cosmetic properties. Expect giant mud slides, mud wrestling, beach concerts, and firework shows.",
    "A cultural festival celebrating Korea's traditional masked performance arts (Talchum). Set in a UNESCO folk village, the performances incorporate satrical storylines mocking historic elites, set to rhythmic drumming.": "A cultural festival celebrating Korea's traditional masked performance arts (Talchum). Set in a UNESCO folk village, the performances incorporate satrical storylines mocking historic elites, set to rhythmic drumming.",
    "The historical stream cutting through downtown Seoul is illuminated by hundreds of massive, intricate paper lanterns crafted from traditional Hanji paper, depicting historical scenes, characters, and futuristic concepts.": "The historical stream cutting through downtown Seoul is illuminated by hundreds of massive, intricate paper lanterns crafted from traditional Hanji paper, depicting historical scenes, characters, and futuristic concepts.",
    "A spectacular and grand fire festival where an entire volcanic hill (Saebyeol Oreum) is set ablaze to pray for health, good harvest, and happiness in the new year, deriving from ancient traditions of burning old grass to clear pests.": "A spectacular and grand fire festival where an entire volcanic hill (Saebyeol Oreum) is set ablaze to pray for health, good harvest, and happiness in the new year, deriving from ancient traditions of burning old grass to clear pests.",
    "One of Korea's most stunning floating lantern festivals. Thousands of beautifully illuminated lanterns float on the Nam River, commemorating the historical defense during the Imjin War (1592) when lanterns were used as military signals and to send messages to families.": "One of Korea's most stunning floating lantern festivals. Thousands of beautifully illuminated lanterns float on the Nam River, commemorating the historical defense during the Imjin War (1592) when lanterns were used as military signals and to send messages to families.",
    "Celebrating Korea's agricultural golden fields, this festival is held at Byeokgolje, the oldest reservoir in Korea. Gimje is the only region in Korea where a clear flat horizon can be seen, symbolizing the abundance of land.": "Celebrating Korea's agricultural golden fields, this festival is held at Byeokgolje, the oldest reservoir in Korea. Gimje is the only region in Korea where a clear flat horizon can be seen, symbolizing the abundance of land.",
    "Set in the breathtaking, terraced green tea plantations of Boseong, this spring festival allows visitors to fully immerse themselves in green tea culture—from picking fresh leaves to making and tasting premium green tea.": "Set in the breathtaking, terraced green tea plantations of Boseong, this spring festival allows visitors to fully immerse themselves in green tea culture—from picking fresh leaves to making and tasting premium green tea.",
    "Recognized as one of the world's seven winter wonders, this freezing festival welcomes millions to catch mountain trout (Sancheoneo) through holes drilled into a 40cm-thick frozen river, or even plunge bare-handed into cold pools.": "Recognized as one of the world's seven winter wonders, this freezing festival welcomes millions to catch mountain trout (Sancheoneo) through holes drilled into a 40cm-thick frozen river, or even plunge bare-handed into cold pools.",
